WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.819
What we're trying to do in the laboratory
is to simulate a process that we think was

00:00:05.819 --> 00:00:07.930
very common on the early Earth.

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:09.849
And it's still common today, in fact.

00:00:09.849 --> 00:00:15.559
Whenever ran falls on a volcano, it makes
little puddles that are heated by the volcanic

00:00:15.559 --> 00:00:16.559
energy.

00:00:16.559 --> 00:00:19.530
When it stops raining, those puddles evaporate.

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:26.410
So we have a wet-dry cycle occurring all the
time on volcanic areas of the present-day

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:27.730
Earth.

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:33.880
In the laboratory, we are simulating that
by building a chamber.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:38.790
The chamber has got to be anaerobic, meaning
there can't be any air in it, because the

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:45.360
oxygen in today's chamber — in today's atmosphere
— will damage the chemicals that we're trying

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:46.489
to work with.

00:00:46.489 --> 00:00:50.220
So we are loading this chamber up with an
inert gas.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:55.600
It happens to be carbon dioxide, but we could
use something like nitrogen if we wanted to.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.390
Carbon dioxide is easy to use.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:01.170
We have a tank sort of blowing CO2 sort of
into the chamber.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:09.049
And as the CO2 comes into contact with the
reaction medium that we are testing, it dries

00:01:09.049 --> 00:01:10.439
it out.

00:01:10.439 --> 00:01:12.750
We have the equivalent of volcanic heat.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:19.850
We have two heaters under a big disk, an aluminum
disk, that is turning very slowly.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:26.710
And every vial that is embedded in this disk,
there's 24 of them, goes through one wet-dry

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:30.070
cycle every 90 minutes.

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:32.790
So that's how we're simulating it.

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:35.929
We're drying it out under a stream of carbon
dioxide.

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:38.710
We're rehydrating with just plain old water.

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:40.850
Then it goes through another drying cycle.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:42.090
This occurs every 90 minute.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:48.579
And in a typical experiment we'll have about
four of these cycles in a day that we will

00:01:48.579 --> 00:01:52.129
then analyze the result and see what we've
made, if anything.

